    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L02-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "2.3: Shrinking Triangles",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Angles \\(C\\) and \\(C’\\) in triangles \\(ABC\\) and \\(A’B’C’\\) are right angles. If \\(\\sin(A) = \\sin(A’)\\), is that sufficient to show that \\(\\triangle ABC\\) is similar to \\(\\triangle A’B’C’\\)? Explain your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l02.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L02-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "2.3: Shrinking Triangles",
      "SourceExcerpt": "In an earlier course, we studied ratios of side lengths in right triangles.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l02.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L02-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "2.3: Shrinking Triangles",
      "SourceExcerpt": "In this triangle, the cosine of angle \\(A\\) is the ratio of the length of the side adjacent to angle \\(A\\) to the length of the hypotenuse—that is \\(\\cos(A) = \\frac{4}{5}\\). The sine of angle \\(A\\) is the ratio of the length of the side…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l02.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L02-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "2.3: Shrinking Triangles",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Now consider triangle \\(A’B’C’\\), which is similar to triangle \\(ABC\\) with a hypotenuse of length 1 unit. Here is a picture of triangle \\(A’B’C’\\) on a coordinate grid:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l02.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L02-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "2.3: Shrinking Triangles",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Since the two triangles are similar, angle \\(A\\) and \\(A'\\) are congruent. So how do the values of cosine, sine, and tangent of these angles compare to the angles in triangle \\(ABC\\)? It turns out that since all three values are ratios of…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l02.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L02-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "2.3: Shrinking Triangles",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Notice that the coordinates of \\(B’\\) are \\(\\left(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\) because segment \\(A’C’\\) has length \\(\\frac{4}{5}\\) and segment \\(B’C’\\) has length \\(\\frac{3}{5}\\). In other words, the coordinates of \\(B’\\) are…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l02.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L02-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "2.3: Shrinking Triangles",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The length of an interval at which a periodic function repeats. A function \\(f\\) has a period, \\(p\\), if \\(f(x+p) = f(x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l02.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L02-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "2.3: Shrinking Triangles",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A function whose values repeat at regular intervals. If \\(f\\) is a periodic function then there is a number \\(p\\), called the period, so that \\(f(x + p) = f(x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l02.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
